The symptom looked like a navigation bug. The robot would run fine while I was connected over SSH, and then, shortly after I closed my last terminal, odometry stopped updating and TF froze. Nav2 saw a robot that had stopped moving and stopped trusting its own position.

Nothing in the ROS logs pointed at the cause, because nothing in ROS had crashed.

## What was actually happening

The ROS 2 stack ran as a normal user, the same user I logged in as. On Ubuntu, `systemd-logind` has a setting called `RemoveIPC`, and it defaults to `yes`. When a user's **last** session ends, logind removes that user's System V and POSIX IPC objects, which includes the files in `/dev/shm`.

Fast DDS, the default ROS 2 middleware, uses shared memory for communication between processes on the same machine. Those segments live in `/dev/shm`. So logging out deleted the shared-memory transport out from under nodes that were still running, and messages between them simply stopped arriving.

That explains the timing: one SSH session open, everything worked; the last one closed, everything froze.

## The fix

Tell logind that this user should keep its resources when no one is logged in:

```bash
sudo loginctl enable-linger <user>
```

A lingering user keeps its user manager running after logout, so its IPC objects are not removed. Other options work too:

- set `RemoveIPC=no` in `/etc/systemd/logind.conf`, or
- run the robot stack as a dedicated system user, which logind does not clean up.

I chose linger because it is one command, per robot, with no change to the stack itself.

## Takeaways

- When a robot fails on a human action (logging out, closing a laptop lid), look at the operating system before the robotics code.
- Shared-memory transports make DDS fast on one machine, and also make it depend on `/dev/shm` behaving.
- Write the fix into the robot's setup so a newly installed robot does not rediscover it.
